course: dictionary
lesson: 7379
title: Words with a story behind them
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words carry a whole picture with them. «The prophet Isaiah dreamed of a world where swords are beaten into ploughshares.» You may not know ploughshare, but the words around it give clues: swords are weapons, and a plough is a farm tool. So the picture is weapons being changed into farm tools.
explain: A ploughshare is the sharp metal blade of a plough that cuts into the soil. Check your guess by putting the meaning into the sentence: «swords are beaten into sharp blades that cut the soil» makes sense as a picture of turning war into farming.
explain: Some phrases come from old stories or religious texts, and they paint a picture. «Beat swords into ploughshares» is a picture of peace. When a phrase does not mean what its words say, think about the picture it paints, then check a dictionary if you need to.
fact: A ploughshare is the sharp metal blade of a plough that cuts into the soil.
fact: «Ploughshare» is a noun, and its plural is «ploughshares». In American English it is spelled «plowshare».
fact: «Swords into ploughshares» comes from the Bible (Isaiah and Micah) and is a picture of peace: weapons are made into farm tools.
fact: Farmers in the past often pulled a plough with oxen or horses.
exercise: What is a ploughshare? | A type of small ship used by sailors | A tall tower for watching the sea | *A sharp metal blade on a plough that cuts into the soil
exercise: «They shall beat their swords into ploughshares.» What does this picture show? | A busy harbour full of ships | Lost treasure buried under the soil | *Peace, with weapons turned into farm tools | War and battles
exercise: «The prophet Isaiah dreamed of a world where swords are beaten into ploughshares.» Find and copy the word that means weapons used in battle. | world | ploughshares | *swords | dreamed
test: What does the phrase «beat swords into ploughshares» mean? | *Turn weapons into tools for farming | Start a new war | Dig a deep hole in the ground | Make a sword very shiny
test: Which part of the plough is the sharp blade that cuts into the soil? | *The ploughshare | The seed bag at the back | The wheel that turns on the ground | The handle you hold
exam: Why might a history lesson show oxen pulling a plough with a ploughshare? | Ploughshares were used to make music | Ploughs were used to fight battles in the past | Oxen were used to carry letters between towns | *Animals pulled ploughs, and the ploughshare cut into the soil to grow food
